PG.notes("l339", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains how running a canister on the live Internet Computer is paid for: ICP tokens are acquired in several ways and burned into non-tradable cycles that power canisters. Introduces the free cycles faucet for learners and warns that cycles are a limited budget.</p>\n<h3>Local simulation vs the live network</h3>\n<ul><li>So far everything ran on a local simulated replica (dfx start) for testing behaviour and latency.</li><li>Deploying to the live network makes the app reachable by anyone and puts it on the ICP blockchain, but it then consumes real computing resources in globally distributed data centres, which must be paid for.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ways to obtain ICP tokens</h3>\n<ul><li>Buy them, or swap another cryptocurrency (such as Bitcoin or Ethereum) for them on a large exchange. This is the simplest route.</li><li>Participate in the Network Nervous System (NNS), the governance system of the Internet Computer: lock existing ICP for a period to create a neuron, then earn rewards by voting on proposals.</li><li>Apply for a developer grant from the Dfinity Foundation. At the time of recording grants ranged from about 25,000 to 100,000 dollars in ICP, with no equity taken.</li><li>Run a node as an independent data centre provider: supplying hardware and electricity for the shared network earns ICP rewards.</li></ul>\n<h3>ICP tokens vs cycles</h3>\n<ul><li>ICP is tradable and can be bought and sold. Cycles are not tradable; they are produced by burning ICP and are credited to a canister.</li><li>A canister needs cycles to compute and store data. While it has enough cycles it stays live; when they run low it must be topped up, similar to refilling a gas bottle at home.</li><li>Think of cycles as pre-loaded computation capacity for each canister.</li></ul>\n<h3>Free cycles for learning</h3>\n<ul><li>Dfinity runs a cycles faucet. At recording time anyone with a GitHub account older than 90 days could claim roughly 20 dollars worth of free cycles, enough to deploy and experiment. The course uses these for its deployments (claimed in the next lesson).</li><li>Warning: once the free allowance is spent it cannot be refilled for free; further cycles require buying ICP and converting it.</li><li>Costs differ by operation. Creating a canister, which is what the first dfx deploy to the network does, is by far the most expensive action (around 0.1 trillion cycles in 2021, somewhat more later). Avoid redeploying carelessly.</li><li>Advice: watch the whole deployment section before running the commands, to conserve cycles.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating free cycles as unlimited: creating canisters is costly and the faucet allowance is one-off.</li><li>Confusing ICP (tradable token) with cycles (non-tradable compute credit).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Figures such as the 20 dollar faucet and grant ranges are dated; check current Dfinity documentation.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l340", "<p class=\"summary\">A step-by-step article for getting free cycles from the Dfinity faucet via Discord, redeeming the coupon into a cycles wallet with dfx, and pointing the project at that wallet. Using cycles is optional for the course: everything can also be run locally.</p>\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n<ul><li>The faucet (updated for 2024 in the course) grants about 10T cycles for free, after some sign-ups. Cycles are only needed to host on the live network; all tutorials work locally without them.</li><li>Flow: faucet website -&gt; Discord bot -&gt; coupon code -&gt; redeem with dfx into a cycles wallet canister -&gt; set that wallet as the project's cycles source.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The faucet process and limits change over time; verify against the current Dfinity faucet page. The lecture page only shows an Open button, so these steps come from the course's reference repo copy of the article.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting the coupon</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the official Dfinity cycles faucet site, choose to request cycles, then accept the invite to the ICP Developer Community Discord server. If the invite fails, log in to Discord first (a free account is needed).</li><li>In Discord go to the #cycles-faucet channel and press the Complete button to accept the rules.</li><li>Type the slash command /request to start the faucet bot, then answer its dropdown survey (Motoko is a fine answer for the language; answers do not change the outcome).</li><li>A popup form (Cycles Faucet Survey) asks for a GitHub account. Submit it; the bot then sends a private message with the coupon code, so Discord DMs must be allowed. Retry or ask in the channel if the form errors.</li></ul>\n<h3>Redeeming the coupon with dfx</h3>\n<ul><li>Back on the faucet page press Next and paste the coupon.</li><li>Check dfx is at least version 0.12.0 with dfx --version; upgrade with sudo dfx upgrade if older.</li><li>Run the redeem command shown by the faucet: dfx wallet --network ic redeem-faucet-coupon followed by the coupon. It creates a cycles wallet canister on the ic network. Write down the wallet id, because it is needed immediately.</li><li>The faucet page offers a verification command to confirm the cycles arrived.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pointing the project at the wallet and registering your device</h3>\n<ul><li>cd into the DBANK project, then run dfx identity --network ic set-wallet --force with the wallet id. This makes the wallet the source of cycles when deploying to the ic network.</li><li>To view the wallet and cycles in the browser UI, authorise your device: get your principal with dfx identity get-principal, then call the wallet canister's authorize method with that principal (dfx canister --network ic call &lt;wallet id&gt; authorize '(principal &quot;&lt;principal id&gt;&quot;)').</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Command sequence for claiming and wiring up the free cycles</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx --version                      # need &gt;= 0.12.0\nsudo dfx upgrade                   # only if older\ndfx wallet --network ic redeem-faucet-coupon &lt;COUPON_CODE&gt;\n# note the wallet id printed here\ncd path/to/DBANK\ndfx identity --network ic set-wallet --force &lt;WALLET_ID&gt;\ndfx identity get-principal\ndfx canister --network ic call &quot;&lt;WALLET_ID&gt;&quot; authorize '(principal &quot;&lt;PRINCIPAL_ID&gt;&quot;)'</pre>\n<h2>The task: Claim free cycles and attach the wallet to DBANK</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A working DBANK project, a Discord account, a GitHub account, and dfx installed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have a funded cycles wallet set as the cycles source for the ic network.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the Dfinity faucet page and request cycles; join the ICP Developer Community on Discord.</li><li>In #cycles-faucet press Complete, then use /request and fill the survey and form.</li><li>Receive the coupon by DM and paste it on the faucet page.</li><li>Verify dfx version (0.12.0 or newer).</li><li>Run dfx wallet --network ic redeem-faucet-coupon with the coupon and record the wallet id.</li><li>Optionally verify the balance with the faucet's check command.</li><li>In the DBANK folder run dfx identity --network ic set-wallet --force with the wallet id.</li><li>Optionally authorise your principal on the wallet to view it online.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The wallet shows a cycles balance and dfx uses it for deployments to the ic network.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Losing the wallet id printed after redeeming.</li><li>Discord DMs disabled, so the coupon never arrives.</li><li>Old dfx version below 0.12.0.</li><li>Running the set-wallet command outside the project or without --network ic.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l341", "<p class=\"summary\">Deploys the DBank project to the live Internet Computer with dfx deploy --network ic, finds the frontend canister's id to build its public URL, and shows why first deployments cost many cycles while later upgrades are cheap.</p>\n<h3>Deploying to the live network</h3>\n<ul><li>Prerequisite: the cycles wallet is funded and linked to the project (previous lecture).</li><li>From inside the project folder run npm install first so the frontend packages are present; if already installed it only takes seconds.</li><li>Run dfx deploy --network ic. The --network flag selects the ic (Internet Computer mainnet) instead of the default local replica. It can take a few minutes.</li><li>The command creates the canisters on the network (paid from the wallet's cycles) and uploads the built frontend assets into the assets canister. It ends by listing the deployed canisters.</li></ul>\n<h3>Finding and opening the live site</h3>\n<ul><li>Get the frontend canister's id with dfx canister --network ic id &lt;assets canister name&gt; (dbank_assets in the lecture; dbank_frontend in the newer template/reference repo).</li><li>Open https://&lt;canister-id&gt;.ic0.app in a browser. This is a normal public URL that anyone with internet access can use.</li><li>Test it by topping up; the calls now run on the real network (the backend query and update calls behave as they did locally, just with real latency).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer docs also serve canisters on .icp0.io, and a canister id domain works the same way.</li></ul>\n<h3>Cycle costs: creating vs upgrading</h3>\n<ul><li>Re-open the cycles wallet page (you may need to log in again): the balance drops noticeably after the first deployment (example: from 14 trillion downwards), because creating a canister is the costliest operation.</li><li>Redeploying after editing code (e.g. commenting out two lines in main.mo) prints that all canisters already exist and only upgrades the code. That is far cheaper.</li><li>Practical rule: avoid creating new canisters unnecessarily; update existing ones.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Deploy to the live Internet Computer and get the site address</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm install\ndfx deploy --network ic\ndfx canister --network ic id dbank_frontend   # prints the frontend canister id\n# open https://&lt;canister-id&gt;.ic0.app</pre>\n<h2>The task: Deploy DBANK to the ICP network</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> DBANK project with working local frontend and a funded cycles wallet set via dfx identity --network ic set-wallet.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have the DBank app reachable at a public canister URL.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project and run npm install.</li><li>Run dfx deploy --network ic and wait for the deployed canisters message.</li><li>Run dfx canister --network ic id with the frontend (assets) canister name to get its id.</li><li>Visit https://&lt;id&gt;.ic0.app and try a top-up.</li><li>Check the wallet balance to see the cost of creating the canisters.</li><li>Make a small edit and redeploy to see the cheaper upgrade path.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The live site works like the local one; the first deploy visibly lowers the cycles balance, and the second deploy reports canisters already created.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting --network ic deploys only to the local replica.</li><li>Deploying fresh canisters repeatedly burns the free cycles.</li><li>Wallet page may require logging in again before showing the balance.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Use your own project's frontend canister name when asking for the id.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l342", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to top up a cycles wallet when the free allowance is gone: obtain ICP from an exchange, send it to your Network Nervous System account, attach the wallet canister there and convert ICP into cycles.</p>\n<h3>When and why you need to convert</h3>\n<ul><li>Cycles run out if you create many canisters or store large files. After that you can only continue locally or acquire ICP.</li><li>ICP is normally bought on a crypto exchange with a local currency; at recording time it was listed on exchanges such as Binance, Coinbase and KuCoin.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture is a demonstration. Buying or trading tokens is a financial decision and optional; the course works fully on the local replica.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demonstrated route to ICP</h3>\n<ul><li>On the exchange used in the demo, a learn-and-earn section pays small token rewards for quizzes about other blockchain projects. The reward (a few dollars in another token) was then swapped to ICP with the exchange's convert feature, giving a tiny ICP amount at the current rate.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Availability of such promotions varies; this is an example, not a guaranteed free path.</li></ul>\n<h3>Moving ICP to the NNS app</h3>\n<ul><li>Log in to the Network Nervous System web app (authenticate with the same identity used for the wallet). Its ICP tab shows your account's wallet address, initially with zero balance.</li><li>In the exchange use send/receive, choose ICP, paste that address and send (the amount can be all of it). Refresh the NNS app until the ICP appears.</li></ul>\n<h3>Converting ICP to cycles</h3>\n<ul><li>In the NNS app open the Canisters tab and choose to create or link a canister, then link your existing cycles wallet by its canister id.</li><li>Find the id with dfx identity --network ic get-wallet in the terminal (or copy it from the wallet page), paste it, and press Attach Canister. This can be slow, and a transient 'not the controller' error may appear and then clear.</li><li>Choose to add cycles to that canister, pick the main account as the source and enter an ICP amount. The review screen shows how many trillion cycles it converts to; confirm and send.</li><li>A minimum amount is required (about 2 trillion cycles worth in the demo).</li><li>Reload the cycles wallet page, authenticate again (a 'register device' prompt may show; refreshing normally fixes it) and the new balance appears, e.g. +2T cycles. The ICP balance in the main account falls by the converted amount.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Find the wallet canister id to attach in the NNS app</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx identity --network ic get-wallet</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Sending ICP to the wrong address; copy the account address from the NNS ICP tab.</li><li>Attach step can be slow and may show a temporary controller error.</li><li>Minimum conversion amount applies.</li><li>Re-authentication or device registration prompts may appear on the wallet page.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l343", "<p class=\"summary\">Hosts a plain static website (the CSS-section demo site) on the Internet Computer: put the site in an assets folder, add a dfx.json describing one assets canister, flatten the file references, deploy with dfx deploy --network ic and open it at &lt;canister-id&gt;.ic0.app.</p>\n<h3>Why host a static site on ICP</h3>\n<ul><li>A small static site (HTML, CSS, a few images; only a couple of hundred KB) is an alternative to a hosting company or GitHub Pages.</li><li>Cost depends on the size of what is stored, so small sites cost very little cycles.</li><li>The lesson uses the course's earlier CSS website; the learner may use their own.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project layout and dfx.json</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a project folder with a subfolder named assets that holds every site file (index.html, favicon, images, stylesheet). This mirrors the assets canister folder of the DBank project.</li><li>Next to (outside) the assets folder, create dfx.json, which tells dfx what canisters exist.</li><li>Inside it, define a canisters object with one canister named website. Its type is 'assets' and its source is an array (several folders are allowed) containing the path to the assets folder.</li><li>The canister name chosen here is the name used later in dfx canister --network ic id website.</li></ul>\n<h3>Flatten the file references</h3>\n<ul><li>The deployment puts all asset files at the same level, collapsing subfolders such as css/ and images/ next to index.html and the favicon. Links with folder prefixes then break.</li><li>Edit index.html (search and replace) to drop the css/ prefix from the stylesheet link and the images/ prefix from the five image sources, leaving bare file names.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo is already flat and uses ./styles.css, ./script.js and ./name.png image paths directly in the assets folder, matching this requirement. It also loads Google Fonts from a CDN, which still works because that is an external URL.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer asset canisters keep folder structure, so this flattening step may not be needed with current dfx versions; test locally first.</li></ul>\n<h3>Deploy and visit</h3>\n<ul><li>Open a terminal in the project folder and run dfx deploy --network ic. This creates the canister (spending wallet cycles) and uploads the assets.</li><li>Get the id with dfx canister --network ic id website, then open https://&lt;id&gt;.ic0.app. The site, including its favicon, loads from the blockchain and can be shared.</li><li>Keep the canister funded: when cycles run out the site can go offline, so top up the canister to keep it available.</li><li>Future course projects stay on local deployment to avoid cost.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">dfx.json for a static site (matches the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;canisters&quot;: {\n    &quot;website&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;assets&quot;,\n      &quot;source&quot;: [&quot;assets&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Folder layout and deploy commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">My Site/\n  dfx.json\n  assets/\n    index.html\n    styles.css\n    favicon.jpg\n    (images ...)\n\ndfx deploy --network ic\ndfx canister --network ic id website\n# open https://&lt;canister-id&gt;.ic0.app</pre>\n<h2>The task: Host a static website on the Internet Computer</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The CSS-section demo site (index.html, CSS folder, images, favicon), downloaded and unzipped, or your own small site.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> The site served live from an ICP canister at its ic0.app address.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Put all site files in a folder called assets inside a project folder.</li><li>Create dfx.json beside assets with a canister called website of type assets with source [&quot;assets&quot;].</li><li>Flatten the references in index.html by removing the css/ and images/ prefixes (five image links).</li><li>Open a terminal in the project folder and run dfx deploy --network ic.</li><li>Run dfx canister --network ic id website to get the canister id.</li><li>Open https://&lt;id&gt;.ic0.app and share the link.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The portfolio-style page loads with its styles, images and favicon from the canister URL.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;canisters&quot;: {\n    &quot;website&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;assets&quot;,\n      &quot;source&quot;: [&quot;assets&quot;]\n    }\n  }\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving folder prefixes in links after the flat upload gives missing CSS and images.</li><li>Running the terminal outside the project folder (it must contain dfx.json).</li><li>Letting the canister run out of cycles takes the site down.</li><li>Wrong canister name in the id command; it must match the name in dfx.json.</li></ul>");
